    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Damascus became prominent as the capital of the Aramaean state of Aram-Damascus, passed through Assyrian, Hellenistic and Roman hands, and was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> from 661 to 750.</li><li>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> moved the seat of power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> it declined, then regained importance under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1401.</li><li>It became the capital of the French Mandate for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1920 and has remained the capital of independent Syria, which became fully independent in 1946.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The English word damask comes from the patterned Byzantine and Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a> that reached the West through Damascus, one of the western termini of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

