    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">965 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Ezron captures Damascus and founds the independent state of Aram-Damascus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">64 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> annexes western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> take Damascus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">634</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> conquers Damascus for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">661</span><span class="rf-text">Mu'awiya makes Damascus the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">750</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> take the city and the seat of Islamic power moves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1401</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> sacks Damascus and burns the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Umayyad Mosque</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1516</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> take Damascus from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920</span><span class="rf-text">A French army enters Damascus, which becomes the capital of the Mandate for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii.jpg" alt="Thutmose III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxor_Museum,_Egypt_49_(trim).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ولاء, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Damascus lies on a plateau 680 metres above sea level, about 80 km inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, sheltered by the Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> mountains and supplied with water by the Barada River. It stood at a crossing of the north-south route linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and the east-west desert route linking Lebanon with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> valley. The river rises from mountain streams fed by melting snow, and the city is surrounded by the Ghouta, irrigated farmland where vegetables, cereals and fruit have been grown since ancient times.</p><p>The city is held to have been first settled in the 3rd millennium BCE. Carbon-14 dating at Tell Ramad, on its outskirts, suggests that the site may have been occupied from the second half of the seventh millennium BCE, possibly around 6300 BCE, and settlement in the wider Barada basin dates to 9000 BCE, but no large-scale settlement lay within Damascus's present walls until the second millennium BCE. Its name appears in the geographical list of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> in the 15th century BCE and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> of about 1350 BCE, where Dimasqu is ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biryawaza">Biryawaza</a>. In Genesis it exists at the time of the War of the Kings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> gave Uz, son of Aram, as its founder. Greek myths gave other founders and explanations of the name Damaskós.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Damascus became an important city with the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans">Aramaeans</a> in the 11th century BCE. They built a water system of canals and tunnels that made the most of the Barada, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> later improved and which still forms the basis of the water supply of the old part of the city. Under the Romans the city prospered during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-romana">Pax Romana</a>. Trade routes from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> converged on it, and it supplied the Roman demand for eastern luxuries. It became a metropolis by the beginning of the 2nd century, and in about 125 Hadrian promoted it to Metropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>.</p><p>Under the Umayyads the city was the capital of an empire stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. From 685 Abd al-Malik introduced a distinctive Islamic coinage and Arabic replaced Greek as the language of administration. The Grand Mosque was begun in 706 and finished by 715, and the city grew beyond its old walls to the slopes of Mount Qasioun. The Ayyubid period brought another phase of expansion, with new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, mosques and hospitals inside and outside the walls, and the district of al-Salihiyah grew on the slopes of the mountain. In the Ottoman period Damascus was the departure point of one of the two great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> caravans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> was more populous and commercially more important for most of those centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/necho-ii.jpg" alt="Necho II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Necho II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Necho-KnellingStatue_BrooklynMuseum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Keith Schengili-Roberts, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Damascus was the capital of Aram-Damascus from 965 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> subdued it in the 8th century BCE, a revolt of 727 BCE was put down, and it later passed into the orbit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Necho II</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> by 572 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered it, and after his death in 323 BCE it passed repeatedly between the Seleucid and Ptolemaic empires. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> annexed western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 64 BCE, and the city became part of the Decapolis and the Roman province of Syria. The Sasanian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrbaraz">Shahrbaraz</a> captured it in 613, and it was in Sasanian hands until near the end of the war of 602 to 628.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> took it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> in 634, and after the Muslim victory on the Yarmouk in 636 Mu'awiya governed it from 639. He made it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> in 661, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan II</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> in 744. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> conquered the city on 25 August 750, and it was then held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a>, Ikhshidids and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. The Seljuqs ruled from 1079, followed by the Burids, Nur ad-Din from 1154 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s Ayyubid line. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> took it in 1260 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1516. The Ottomans stayed for about 400 years, apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-pasha-of-egypt">Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt</a>'s occupation from 1832 to 1840.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan.jpg" alt="Mimar Sinan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mimar_Sinan_signature.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mimar Sinan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Old City keeps the rectangular shape of the Roman city, about 1,500 by 750 metres inside a wall with seven gates, of which only the eastern gate, Bab Sharqi, kept its Roman form. Its two main axes were the Decumanus Maximus, now the Via Recta or Straight Street, and the Cardo. Roman Damascus lies mostly up to five metres below the modern city. The old borough of Bab Tuma grew at the end of the Roman and Byzantine era around the local Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> community, and according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> Saint Paul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-u-s-virgin-islands">Saint Thomas</a> both lived there. When Muslim armies took the city the population remained mostly Christian, with a growing Muslim community.</p><p>The Seljuqs reversed Shia gains in the city and expanded religious life through private endowments that paid for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and hospitals, and Damascus became an important centre of Islamic thought. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> the Al-Murshidiyya, Al-Jaharkasiyya, Al-Sahiba, Al-Shibliyya, Al-Rukniyya and Al-Shamiyya madrasas were founded. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>, allowed to practise their religions under restrictions. An Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 10,423 households in the early 16th century, and a missionary put the population at about 140,000 in 1867, of whom 30,000 were Christians and 10,000 Jews. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a> designed the Sulaymaniyya Takiyya for pilgrims on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, completed in 1559.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque.jpg" alt="Umayyad Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Umayyad Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umayyad_Mosque,_Damascus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Damascus suffered many sieges and disasters. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> plundered the kingdom outside its walls in the 9th century BCE. After the Abbasid conquest in 750 the Umayyad family cemetery was desecrated and the city walls were torn down. On 24 November 847 a multiple earthquake struck and destroyed Damascus, killing about 70,000 people. In 998 the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Hakim executed hundreds of its citizens for incitement. In 1129 about 6,000 Isma'ili Muslims were killed in the city after rumours of a plot, and in 1148 a crusader siege failed.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> entered on 1 March 1260 and Ghazan besieged the city in 1299 to 1300. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348 to 1349 killed as much as half of the population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> besieged Damascus in 1400 and sacked it on 17 March 1401. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Umayyad Mosque</a> was burnt, men and women were enslaved and many of the artisans were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. Many other people were slaughtered, and their heads were piled outside the north-east corner of the walls, at a place still called Burj al-Ru'us, the tower of heads. In June 1860 several thousand Christians were killed when fighting between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronites</a> spilled over from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>, and the Christian quarter was burnt. The Algerian exile Abd al-Qadir and his soldiers sheltered many others. In 1926 French bombing and shelling during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-syrian-revolt">Great Syrian Revolt</a> burned the area between Al-Hamidiyah and Medhat Pasha souqs, known since as al-Hariqa, the fire. Reporter George Seldes counted 308 bodies and suggested that the maximum might be one thousand.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Umayyad Mosque</a>, begun in 706 on the site of a Byzantine church dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, a Hellenistic temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and a Roman temple of Jupiter, is one of the oldest sites of continuous prayer since the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. A shrine inside is said to contain the body of Saint John the Baptist, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s mausoleum stands in the gardens outside. The Roman layout of the Old City, the Straight Street named in Acts 9:11, the Citadel and the Ottoman and Ayyubid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and mosques also survive. The Old City was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1979.</p><p>The city's name lives on in the English word damask. In the 20th century nationalist feeling grew against Ottoman rule, and Jamal Pasha hanged a number of patriotic intellectuals in 1915 and 1916. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a> and British forces approached Damascus in 1918, and Faisal ibn Hussein was proclaimed king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. A French army defeated a small Syrian force at Maysalun in 1920 and made Damascus the capital of the Mandate for Syria. The French agreed to leave in 1946. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-civil-war">Syrian civil war</a> fighting reached the outskirts of the city in 2012, the Ghouta suburb was bombed in 2017 and 2018, and rebels entered Damascus on 8 December 2024. The 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave the city 1,552,161 people, and it is the centre of a metropolitan area estimated at 5 million.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Damascus, on the Barada below the Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> mountains, was the capital of Aram-Damascus, a Roman city and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> from 661 to 750. It was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1401 and became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Its Old City is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</em></p>

